From: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Date: Tue, 22 Sep 2026 01:21:41 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] hw/virtio-media: return the OUTPUT buffer timestamp on completion

With 0219 the stateful handshake completes on the board: the guest renegotiates
to 1920x1080 and QBUF never fails. Playback still produced no picture, and the
decoder went quiet holding every output buffer it had.

Instrumenting C2SurfaceSyncObj in the guest said where it ended up
(isDequeueable=false, max=23, cur=23, status=1): the pool had handed out all
23 buffers the surface allows and none had come back, with the sync object
healthy and ACTIVE -- not a surface problem, and not
a buffer-count one. The same run logged, once per input buffer from the second
on:

    W V4L2Decoder: Callback is already abandoned.

That message is about the INPUT queue. v4l2_codec2 carries its bitstreamId in
the OUTPUT buffer's timestamp and looks the decode callback up by it when the
buffer completes (V4L2Decoder.cpp):

    int32_t id = dequeuedBuffer->getTimeStamp().tv_sec;
    auto it = mPendingDecodeCbs.find(id);
    if (it == mPendingDecodeCbs.end()) {
        ALOGW("Callback is already abandoned.");
        continue;
    }

and we were completing OUTPUT buffers with pts_us = -1. dec_complete_buffer()
only fills the timestamp when pts_us >= 0, so every input buffer came back with
tv_sec = 0: bitstreamId 0 matched, and every later one looked up an entry that
had already been erased. Those callbacks are the only way the component learns
its input was consumed, so CCodec stopped recycling, the output buffers were
never returned to the surface, the pool saturated, and fetchGraphicBlock()
blocked for good. Software decode on the same gallery3d SurfaceView played
normally, which is what pointed the search back here.

So echo it. This is not a workaround for one client: V4L2 specifies that the
timestamp travels with the buffer, and for a decoder that it is copied from the
OUTPUT buffer to the CAPTURE buffer produced from it -- which dec_deliver
already does via fr->pts, since libavcodec carries the packet pts through. The
OUTPUT side was simply the half that was never wired up.

Both input completions are fixed, the normal one and the empty-buffer flush: a
client that signals EOS with a zero-length buffer expects that buffer back the
same way.

Assisted-by: Claude Code:claude-opus-5
Signed-off-by: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [depends on the downstream virtio-media device
 (0216), which upstream qemu does not have]
---
diff --git a/hw/virtio/virtio-media-decoder.c b/hw/virtio/virtio-media-decoder.c
index 85f5e990b11..9fbb7d2c076 100644
--- a/hw/virtio/virtio-media-decoder.c
+++ b/hw/virtio/virtio-media-decoder.c
@@ -109,6 +109,33 @@
 /* Frames one dec_pump() call may deliver; see the loop for why it is bounded. */
 #define DEC_PUMP_MAX_FRAMES 4
 
+/*
+ * THE TIMESTAMP OF AN OUTPUT BUFFER MUST COME BACK WITH IT.
+ *
+ * Completing an OUTPUT buffer with pts_us = -1 leaves dec_complete_buffer's
+ * timestamp at zero, so the guest gets tv_sec = 0 for every input buffer it
+ * queues. v4l2_codec2 carries its bitstreamId in exactly that field and looks
+ * the decode callback up by it:
+ *
+ *     int32_t id = dequeuedBuffer->getTimeStamp().tv_sec;
+ *     auto it = mPendingDecodeCbs.find(id);
+ *     if (it == mPendingDecodeCbs.end()) { ALOGW("Callback is already abandoned."); continue; }
+ *
+ * so bitstreamId 0 matches and every later one does not (measured on the
+ * board without this: "Callback is already abandoned" once per input buffer
+ * from the second on). Those callbacks are how the component learns its input
+ * was consumed, so CCodec stops recycling, the output buffers are never
+ * returned to the surface, and the pool saturates (instrumented
+ * C2SurfaceSyncObj: max=23 cur=23, status ACTIVE), which stalls
+ * fetchGraphicBlock in C2_BLOCKING for good.
+ *
+ * V4L2 is explicit that the timestamp travels with the buffer (and from the
+ * OUTPUT buffer to the CAPTURE buffer decoded from it), so echoing it is not a
+ * workaround for this one client.
+ */
+#define DEC_IN_PTS(b) \
+    ((int64_t)(b)->timestamp.tv_sec * 1000000 + (b)->timestamp.tv_usec)
+
 /*
  * What the input backlog may hold. Bounded for the reason DEC_MAX_OUT_SIZE is:
  * the guest decides how much it queues, so a guest that feeds input while never
@@ -1253,7 +1280,7 @@ static int dec_qbuf(VirtIOMediaSession *s, uint8_t *payload, size_t payload_len)
                 d->drain_done = false;
                 avcodec_send_packet(d->avctx, NULL);
             }
-            dec_complete_buffer(s, 0, b->index, 0, 0, -1);
+            dec_complete_buffer(s, 0, b->index, 0, 0, DEC_IN_PTS(b));
             dec_pump(s);
             return 0;
         }
@@ -1357,7 +1384,7 @@ static int dec_qbuf(VirtIOMediaSession *s, uint8_t *payload, size_t payload_len)
          * is reported before any decoded frame, which is the order a stateful
          * decoder gives: input completion does not wait for output.
          */
-        dec_complete_buffer(s, 0, b->index, (uint32_t)used, 0, -1);
+        dec_complete_buffer(s, 0, b->index, (uint32_t)used, 0, DEC_IN_PTS(b));
     }
 
     dec_pump(s);
